        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
When changes in the biomechanical environment of the circulation occur, blood vessels undergo well-orchestrated structural and metabolic remodeling to restore optimal function. The investigators proposed that this remarkable adaptive ability lies at the center of orthostatic intolerance exhibited by most astronauts on return to earth’s gravitational field after modest-to-long periods in microgravity. Investigators therefore mapped gene expression (transcription profiling) of the different vascular steady states exhibited in vivo (mouse) in simulated hypergravity and microgravity, and the transitions between them, in order to design better countermeasures for undesired vascular consequences in long-term space flight. The central objective of this project was to develop in a well-defined mouse model, a reference databank of gene expression profiles of the normal mammalian vasculature as a function of gravitational history and gravitational transition using RNA amplification technology developed to detect quantitative differences in multiple gene expressions in the heart, vasculature, and lungs. Refinement to small amounts of tissues, RNA amplification, and array technology was used to study many genes simultaneously and a well-developed bioinformatics program ensured efficient data analysis, annotation, and dissemination. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for the study isn’t available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Fidelity analyses of expression profiling of very small amounts of tissues/cells is a developing technology of immense value for high-throughput site specific phenotyping. Findings support the ‘high risk’ conviction that the techniques, essential for the gravity experiments, will provide a comprehensive profile of cardiovascular gene expression for biomedicine in general.
<p>
        </div>

